 there is showing you a time that lily  has gotten up at night and this is over  a year and a half of of nights and what  we did was we found an app on our iPad  and it's actually a pregnancy app for  contractions and what we would do is  every time she would awaken we would hit  the button when we leave our room and go  into her room and help her and once she  got settled back to sleep we would go  back into our room and turn it off  so we were able to see where these  pockets were of when she was sleeping  really badly and when she was just  sleeping simply badly but it was really  the first time that anyone had collected  any kind of data and we you know we just  decided as a family we needed to start  collecting data so we could help the  researchers see the severity of the the  awakenings at night  so we were fortunate enough to be able  to take part in the idiopathic diseases  of man study that was run by Scripps  translational science Institute and this  is when Lily was 15 years old she was  luckily admitted into this study and we  were able to get the results from this  but when we were going through it we  went through another study at the same  time which was the emotional aspect of  having your whole genome sequenced and I  specifically remember one of the  questions that they asked Lily when we  were we had already had our blood drawn  and we were waiting for the results and  one of the questions that the  researchers wanted to know was you know  what if what if something really bad was  found and something that was terminal  and that they could do nothing about and  Lily was never afraid of that and  actually I wasn't either it was I think  it's important for physicians to know  who are who have the ability to offer  whole genome sequencing to families that  actually finding out bad news like that  is something that you can process and  you can move forward with but not having  a diagnosis is actually more difficult  and Lily's biggest fear of going through  this process and then not having any  diagnosis at the end that was actually  more upsetting to her to think about  then getting something back that would  be you know something someone would  consider to be very challenging  so when when we did get the results back  from the whole genome sequence II we  were given two genes that had mutations  on them one is the ABC Y 5 gene and the  other sadoc 3 gene and one they told us  the ABC y5 gene they could possibly do  something about the Dok 3 gene was  something that they didn't have any data  on as of yet and both of these genes are  responsible for balance and coordination  and they've been described to me as kind  of the gears on a bike and if the gears  don't work the whole bike's not going to  go so when we were given this  information we were also told that she  would have a normal life expectancy  which that was big news to us because  that was not something that we were we  were not given that before she had the  diagnosis so the first thing that we did  was we were able to take Lily off of all  of her seizure medications which  obviously you can imagine would be  debilitating during the day as they made  her tired and they just kind of made  things a little bit better with the  sleeping but they never alleviated  everything fully so we were able to  start a drug called Diamox and that  enables Lily to sleep for about nine  days in a row nine nights and we had not  slept that you know we hadn't slept a  full night in 15 years so that was  absolutely life-changing after the nine  days and nights we started seeing the  sleep fall away and the tremors were  coming back and we were able to adjust  the medication enough and we've also  added tetra Ben Azim and Lily now sleeps  all night she doesn't have any issues  sometimes stress will bring things back  for a week but she quickly gets back on  track and  we've been able to you know change  things pretty significantly for her so a  lot of people have said to us well you  know she's still in a wheelchair and  what's the benefit but I can tell you  that sleeping and being able to sleep  all night  is a huge benefit and life-changing and  you think being in a wheelchair is bad  it's actually worse if you can't sleep  at night so just to let you know Lily's  life changed she was able to we could  take her and stay in hotel as you can  imagine we couldn't stay in a hotel  before with her being up all night  crying and we took her to Paris in  London for her graduation Christmas  birthday combination gift and we were  able to stay in hotel there with her and  she could see all kinds of beautiful art  that she loves and she was really able  to experience those cities fully she  also is now away at college and although  she needs 24/7 care she is you know as  I've explained cognitively typical and  she's been able to do things that we  never imagined that she would be able to  do so after this diagnosis we shared our  story with a lot of different places  that asked us to and the main reason  that we share our story so much is that  we want to give other families hope and  we want people to understand what we  went through and what other families  might be enduring and they don't know  that they have this mutation and if  anybody can go online in Google Ad cy5  they will find us and they can connect  with us quickly and we can hopefully get  them in contact with a specialist who  can help them with the sleep issues and  we can also let them know that know the  science that we're doing  so now on to other things you get your  diagnosis and you think your Odysseys  over and actually you're just beginning  a whole new journey because now you have  to figure out what to do with that  information that you have  so after sequencing what we did was we  kind of assessed where where lily was  and with the sleeping everything was  much better but whole genome g-code  whole genome sequencing doesn't always  give you a treatment it might give you  the answer it might give you some ideas  but it might not give you the answers  that you need to completely be relieved  of your diagnosis and even though  everything is better for Lily a lot of  things are better there's still things  that she has to endure and the reason  that we want to continue the science she  still gets a monthly IVIG treatment  which is an infusion that is actually  ten hours long she has it five hours on  one day and five hours on the next she  takes an orphan drug every day that  twice a day it's over six thousand  dollars a month  she has 24/7 care and she you know  continues to be reliant on wheelchairs  and other durable medical equipment and  you know things that help her get  through her day  so what we've done is we've we've  started a DC y5 org and we did that  shortly after a diagnosis and the main  purpose of that is to support other  families we also want to be able to  provide a place for researchers and  clinicians to go if they have a  diagnosis or interesting in the science  we want to be able to give them a place  to look and see what's happening and be  able to connect with each other and what  we did as a family is we put all this  together so that we can make it as easy  as possible for researchers and  clinicians to get the information use  the information in order to help other  parents  so we're working together to really  leverage technology and put things  together ourselves so that we can help  the researchers do what they do best and  what we don't know how to do as parents  so we have put together a family meeting  where we were able to get clinicians and  researchers together in one room and we  could put seven different families in  front of them one at a time and they  could see them one after the other and  compare and contrast and take notes and  we've been able to work with these  clinicians to put together different  ideas so that we can start doing a  Natural History study and and others and  other science in order to move toward  treatment so when our when the people  that we've worked with before Lily was  diagnosed it was mostly institutions  around the country now the connections  that we have are much more people who  are in the science field and technology  and are doing things that are moving  towards science and treatment  we share our story with anyone who will  listen to us speak and we I do a lot of  public speaking and I will speak to  anyone from a local organization who's  just interested in science and what's  happening today to the different  universities in our area and different  corporations who are involved in science  just to let them know the benefits that  we've had by learning about science  including people who work with science  in our disease  so through this Lily was she was the  first one to be diagnosed with the  mutation that encompassed her whole body  and since then we've been able to  connect with over 80 families that have  since gotten the same diagnosis that  Lily has and in the papers there are  over 300 people who have been identified  in different databases and because this  is such a small population we have taken  it upon ourselves to put together the  science and really try to make some  breakthroughs with this and one of the  things that we've been doing is the IPSC  project and we were the first ones as a  small rare disease organization to put  together a stem cell project for the  ultra rare disease community we've been  able to get 13 lines of patients who  have the actual ad cy5 mutation and also  31 parents and siblings who are not  affected by the mutation and these cells  are available through choreo and what  this means is that scientists have  access to these at a much lower price  and they're available all over the world  and the impact that we have for you know  for the scientists is if we can provide  these IPSC to them rather than saying to  them we can provide blood or skin and  then they in turn have to turn them into  these stem cells it completely lowers  the price for the scientists they now  can get them as stem cell lines they  know that they can get more than one  stem cell line to compare them we have  been able to take this project and offer  the results to scientists so that they  can abbreviate the work and the funding  that they need to move the project  forward we're also able to offer this  with no IT reach back conditions so they  can go ahead and do the work and get  credit for what they come up with and  what this does is it it enables the you  know the true precision medicine efforts  it's taking the IPS sees of these of  these patients and giving the scientists  the resources that they need to move  forward toward a treatments  so the next step that we're taking as a  foundation is reaching out to different  scientists and letting them know that  these stem cell lines are available  we're also putting together a Natural  History study so that we can have  retrospective and prospective data on  all these patients and using different  sensors that so that we can collect the  information for patients who are still  not sleeping well at night and educating  different people who go to conferences  about AV cy5 related dyskinesia and just  letting them know about the projects  that we have put together and that we  have available and that they can  participate in and then we're also  working to identify funding and and we  are funding a researcher right now to  continue his work so that we make sure  that the treatment is coming faster than  it might have if we weren't putting  these things together  so we we've been able to abbreviate the  time and also the money for these  researchers they can just get right down  to work with the IPSC cells  so these are our next steps as an  organization of what we plan to do with  these cells that we were able to get  we're going to be differentiating them  doing RNA sequencing and reviewing  different proposals for model organisms  and then as I discussed doing the  Natural History study so that we can  collect as much information that week as  we can about the disease so that we can  forward that on to researchers so that  they can utilize the information  there are a lot of benefits to having  these stem cells it's been able to help  us attract different researchers because  we already have this tool for them to  use and we're we're seeing benefits  already many of you have seen the movie  with John Travolta about the boy in the  bubble and you know that was something  that was a debilitating disease in the  past and now if that mutation is shown  to be present for a newborn baby they  can get the treatment and they have a  tough couple of years of building that  immune system but once they build it  there they're cured from the disease and  this is the kind of thing that we have  in our mind for the future for Lily and  for other children and adults who have  been diagnosed with AV cy5 related  dyskinesia  so people always ask you know where is  Lily now and what is she doing she's a  senior in college she goes to Whittier  College up by Los Angeles it's a small  liberal arts school easy to get around  because of the nice small campus and  it's been great for Lily to take part in  the campus she's majoring in political  science and hasn't has will have a minor  in English and she's very involved in  the student body she was in the Student  Senate she was a member of a sorority  and she is now a football and basketball  cheerleader so she is you know very  happy there and very interested in the  science and we have conference calls she  wants to be on the call to listening to  hear what's happening and it really it  gives her hope and it lets her know that  she is a pioneer in the science and that  everything that that we put together as  an organization we put together for her  will someday affect positively other  people who are affected with not only  her mutation but other mutations because  we believe that anything that we're  learning about you know through using  these stem cells will somehow benefit  everybody in rare disease and that is  our goal is to move treatment forward  for everybody  if you're interested in contacting me  about questions my email is Gigi ro SS  ma n @ ad cy5 org and you can also  either google Lily or myself and read a  lot of different stories about our  family and what we have accomplished and  what we hope to accomplish and if you  have any questions then I welcome you to  email me at any time and I will get back  to you but thank you so much for your  time and good luck with the research  that you plan to do okay
 

